On 8/14/2019 7:24 PM, Mark E. Shoulson wrote:
> Not quite directly relevant to this, but also regarding hyphens:=C2=A0 I=
=20
> recall long LONG ago, when I was making a lujvo of vofli+litru, (to=20
> express just "flying along"; it looks like vofli alone would have worked=
=20
> okay, but it may have had a different place structure then, or maybe I=20
> just didn't realize it.=C2=A0 Not the point), with the rafsi -voi- plus=
=20
> -li'u-, obviously I need a consonant hyphen to get the required=20
> consonant cluster, but the rules stipulated that it *had* to be -r-,=20
> thus, voirli'u. which at least to my articulatory organs felt quite=20
> awkward.=C2=A0 It winds up being closer to 4 syllables than 3; the -r-=20
> becomes vocalic, or nearly so.=C2=A0 Not so if I had been allowed to say=
=20
> *voinli'u.=C2=A0 Is there a reason to forbid giving the speaker a choice=
=20
> regarding the consonant-hyphen, given that there are only two(?) allowed=
=20
> anyway?

The original reason for having a rule on which consonant to use was so=20
that there would be a rule, and words would be spelled one canonical way=20
that could be implemented by computer (CLL section 4.11).  The rule also=20
required use of the shortest form by a specific scoring algorithm=20
(section 4.12).  The lujvo-making and scoring algorithm predates the=20
split between TLI Loglan and Lojban - JCB and I formulated it in May 1986.

Historically, however, some people wanted to not have to use the=20
specific scoring algorithm, and we now recognize multiple spellings of=20
the "same word" can exist using longer rafsi choices (e.g. voflylitru).=20
So I personally have no problem with using a different hyphen. I don't=20
think the morphology algorithm gives a problem (other than the general=20
rule that fu'ivla space is to be exclusive of lujvo space, which might=20
technically cause someone to think that voinli'u was a fu'ivla because=20
if it were a lujvo it would be voirli'u).

I think at the time, i actually liked the idea of having the hyphen=20
vocalized as a separate syllable, but that is probably personal aesthetics.

A formal change to allow this (and to state it as such in CLL) should=20
still require official approval by LLG (either a byfy-like group or the=20
Board.

lojbab

